WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.299 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The canteen has a list of snack votes, but “apple” and
“Apple” appear as different choices.

00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:07.449 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Will the graph tell the right story?

00:00:07.449 --> 00:00:10.982 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine six snack cards scattered on a canteen desk.

00:00:10.982 --> 00:00:15.061 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Some say apple with a capital letter and one uses a small
letter.

00:00:15.061 --> 00:00:21.671 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Sorting the cards should help us understand the votes, but a
computer may need us to use a consistent spelling first.

00:00:21.671 --> 00:00:24.310 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Good displays begin with careful checking.

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:29.281 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are learning to turn checked responses into a
trustworthy frequency display.

00:00:29.281 --> 00:00:34.294 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our goal is to turn responses into a trustworthy frequency
table and display.

00:00:34.294 --> 00:00:37.835 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We are looking for a process someone else could follow and
check.

00:00:37.835 --> 00:00:44.016 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A beautiful graph is not useful if the counts came from
missing, mistaken or double-counted records.

00:00:44.016 --> 00:00:47.515 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can spot missing, invalid and repeated records.

00:00:47.515 --> 00:00:51.893 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can make a frequency table that counts every valid
response once.

00:00:51.893 --> 00:00:55.645 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can use a digital table or spreadsheet to represent data.

00:00:55.645 --> 00:01:00.042 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You already know how to sort objects into groups and count
each group.

00:01:00.042 --> 00:01:03.611 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today each card will represent one pupil’s response.

00:01:03.611 --> 00:01:07.128 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Think of the table as a tidy record of those groups.

00:01:07.128 --> 00:01:12.389 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each response must contribute exactly once, and the total
gives us an important check.

00:01:12.389 --> 00:01:16.524 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Frequency means how often a category or value occurs.

00:01:16.524 --> 00:01:21.684 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Validate means check the data against the question and
collection rules before counting.

00:01:21.684 --> 00:01:28.033 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use a consistent spelling, check missing answers with the
collector, and investigate duplicate pupil records.

00:01:28.033 --> 00:01:31.618 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Matching answers from different pupils are not duplicates.

00:01:31.618 --> 00:01:34.344 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Never quietly invent a missing answer.

00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:38.118 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Collecting data and checking data are connected jobs.

00:01:38.118 --> 00:01:42.324 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
First agree what choices are allowed and what one record
represents.

00:01:42.324 --> 00:01:47.327 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then look for unexpected words, empty spaces and repeated
pupil records.

00:01:47.327 --> 00:01:51.431 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If a response is unclear, ask the collector or pupil to
check it.

00:01:51.431 --> 00:01:54.518 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We must not guess which snack a blank answer meant.

00:01:54.518 --> 00:01:59.524 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six different pupils answered: Apple, Banana, apple, Pear,
Apple, Banana.

00:01:59.524 --> 00:02:01.580 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How many valid votes are there?

00:02:01.580 --> 00:02:04.271 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Look at the six cards before we sort them.

00:02:04.271 --> 00:02:06.779 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict the total number of votes.

00:02:06.779 --> 00:02:10.257 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Then look for cards that belong to the same snack group.

00:02:10.257 --> 00:02:16.693 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Take a moment to explain why matching snack choices from
different pupils should still remain in our collection.

00:02:21.693 --> 00:02:25.728 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Standardise apple to Apple: Apple 3, Banana 2, Pear 1.

00:02:25.728 --> 00:02:29.236 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The total is 3 + 2 + 1 = 6.

00:02:29.236 --> 00:02:32.757 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
All six votes count; repeated snack choices are normal.

00:02:32.757 --> 00:02:38.241 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Place the first Apple card in its group, then add the
small-letter apple and the final Apple.

00:02:38.241 --> 00:02:40.115 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
That group has three cards.

00:02:40.115 --> 00:02:42.575 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Banana has two and Pear has one.

00:02:42.575 --> 00:02:45.302 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Write those frequencies beside their labels.

00:02:45.302 --> 00:02:50.674 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Adding three, two and one gives six, matching the number of
pupil responses we started with.

00:02:50.674 --> 00:02:53.950 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Put one vote in each cell A2 to A7.

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:55.853 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
In C2 type Apple.

00:02:55.853 --> 00:03:00.046 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What does =COUNTIF(A2:A7,C2) find?

00:03:00.046 --> 00:03:04.360 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine moving the cards into a spreadsheet, one checked
vote per row.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:07.238 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Put the category Apple in a separate cell.

00:03:07.238 --> 00:03:11.123 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Before using a counting tool, predict the number it should
return.

00:03:11.123 --> 00:03:15.986 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What would you check if the result were different from the
three Apple cards you sorted?

00:03:20.986 --> 00:03:23.306 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It counts the cells matching Apple.

00:03:23.306 --> 00:03:27.792 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use a row for each category and check that the frequencies
sum to six.

00:03:27.792 --> 00:03:34.205 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A column graph has separated equal-width bars, category
labels and a frequency scale starting at zero.

00:03:34.205 --> 00:03:38.838 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The spreadsheet’s count-if formula counts the cells matching
a chosen category.

00:03:38.838 --> 00:03:44.414 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It can help make the frequency table, but we still need to
check spelling and the selected range of cells.

00:03:44.414 --> 00:03:48.405 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To draw a column graph, use one separated column for each
snack.

00:03:48.405 --> 00:03:53.407 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Start the frequency scale at zero, label the categories, and
use equal intervals.

00:03:53.407 --> 00:03:59.321 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw Apple’s column to three pupils, Banana’s column to two
pupils and Pear’s column to one pupil.

00:03:59.321 --> 00:04:05.203 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
“Remove every repeated answer.” Only repeated records for
the same pupil need checking.

00:04:05.203 --> 00:04:08.047 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Different pupils may choose the same snack.

00:04:08.047 --> 00:04:12.851 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If two pupils both choose Banana, that is useful information
about the class.

00:04:12.851 --> 00:04:15.549 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Removing one would change the evidence.

00:04:15.549 --> 00:04:21.085 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If the same pupil record appears twice, investigate whether
it is a recording mistake.

00:04:21.085 --> 00:04:27.008 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The response value and the record identity answer different
questions, so they need different checks.

00:04:27.008 --> 00:04:31.065 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Pause the player and try the investigation in the written
lesson.

00:04:31.065 --> 00:04:35.667 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Make a prediction, collect or change the data, then explain
what you notice.

00:04:35.667 --> 00:04:37.656 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Try another kind of data.

00:04:37.656 --> 00:04:42.297 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For an interest rating, keep low, medium and high in that
meaningful order.

00:04:42.297 --> 00:04:46.877 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For books borrowed, use whole-number counts such as zero,
one, two, three and four.

00:04:46.877 --> 00:04:53.877 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep a zero-frequency row when no pupil has a particular
count, so the display shows the gap rather than hiding it.

00:04:53.877 --> 00:04:58.635 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain one check you made before counting and one way to
check the finished table.

00:04:58.635 --> 00:05:01.011 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Compare your checked table and graph.

00:05:01.011 --> 00:05:04.256 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does every table value match its column height?

00:05:04.256 --> 00:05:07.869 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Does the total match the number of valid responses?

00:05:07.869 --> 00:05:11.038 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Can a reader tell what was counted and who answered?

00:05:11.038 --> 00:05:17.772 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A saved frequency table can be opened in a spreadsheet,
giving another way to check and represent the same evidence.

00:05:17.772 --> 00:05:24.999 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An organised display should help another person understand
the responses without seeing your original pile of cards.

00:05:24.999 --> 00:05:29.647 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Which validation check mattered most today, and how could
you explain its purpose?

00:05:29.647 --> 00:05:34.861 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Start with checked responses, count each one once, and use
the total to help test your work.
